<main class="container">
<article>
<section class="hero">
<h1>
The 10 Biggest Hotels in the World by Number of Rooms
</h1>
<p class="subtitle">
Room count is the bluntest measure of hotel scale — and the most
revealing. These 10 properties, all currently open and operational, run
more rooms than most cities have hotels. Here's what the list looks like,
and what running a property at this scale actually takes.
</p>
<p class="byline">
By <strong>Nigel A. Thomas</strong><br/>
Hospitality Executive &amp; Corporate Trainer
</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Why Room Count Still Matters</h2>
<p>
Revenue management has taught the industry to think in RevPAR, GOPPAR and
ADR — and rightly so, those are the numbers that decide whether a
hotel is profitable. But raw room count still tells you something none of
those ratios do: the sheer operational complexity a property has to absorb
every single day. A 200-room hotel and a 7,000-room hotel aren't running
the same business at different sizes — they're running fundamentally
different operations.
</p>
<p>
The 10 hotels below are the largest on the planet by room inventory, all
currently open and operational. Seven are casino resorts, three are
integrated entertainment complexes, and every one of them has had to solve
housekeeping, staffing, F&amp;B and guest-flow problems at a scale most
GMs will never encounter in a career.
</p>
<div class="card">
<h3>What You Will Learn</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 10 largest hotels in the world, ranked by room count.</li>
<li>Where each one is located and what makes it distinctive.</li>
<li>The difference between a "largest hotel complex" and a "largest single hotel building."</li>
<li>What operating at this scale actually demands from management.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
