<section id="rankings">
<h2>The Ranking</h2>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank1">
<div class="rank-number">1</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>First World Hotel</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Genting Highlands, Malaysia • <strong>7,351 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Part of Resorts World Genting, First World Hotel is officially recognized
by Guinness World Records as the largest hotel in the world by room count.
It sits on a mountaintop resort complex above Kuala Lumpur, combining
accommodation with a full indoor theme park, casino and shopping mall
under one roof — a property built less around a single guest
experience and more around an entire self-contained entertainment
destination.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank2">
<div class="rank-number">2</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>The Venetian Resort</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Las Vegas, Nevada, USA • <strong>7,264 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Combining The Venetian, The Palazzo and the Venetian North Tower into one
operating complex, this is the largest hotel complex in the world by total
square footage and among the largest by room count. Its Venice-themed
canals, gondola rides and connected convention space make it as much an
events and MICE powerhouse as an accommodation property — a model
many integrated resorts have since tried to replicate.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank3">
<div class="rank-number">3</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>Abraj Al Bait</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Makkah, Saudi Arabia • <strong>6,000 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Overlooking the Masjid al-Haram, Abraj Al Bait is part of the world's
tallest clock tower complex and exists to serve one of the most
predictable, high-density demand patterns in global hospitality —
pilgrims arriving for Hajj and Umrah. Its scale isn't driven by
entertainment or convention business, but by religious tourism at a volume
few other markets on Earth experience.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank4">
<div class="rank-number">4</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>The Londoner Macao</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Cotai, Macau • <strong>6,000 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Themed around London landmarks — complete with a Big Ben replica and
a recreation of the Houses of Parliament — The Londoner Macao is part
of the Sands Resorts Cotai Strip complex. It represents Macau's approach to
integrated resort design: casino gaming as the revenue anchor, with hotel
rooms, retail and entertainment built around it at a scale matched almost
nowhere outside Las Vegas.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank5">
<div class="rank-number">5</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>MGM Grand Las Vegas</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Las Vegas, Nevada, USA • <strong>5,044 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
MGM Grand holds a distinction none of the properties above it can claim:
it's the largest <em>single hotel building</em> in the world. Where First
World Hotel and The Venetian Resort reach their totals across connected
towers and complexes, MGM Grand's 5,044 rooms sit under one roof —
making it, in operational terms, the single most concentrated hotel
building on the planet.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank6">
<div class="rank-number">6</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>Izmailovo Hotel</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Moscow, Russia • <strong>5,000 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Built for the 1980 Moscow Summer Olympics, Izmailovo Hotel was designed
from the outset to solve a single problem: housing enormous numbers of
visitors for a fixed-duration global event. Its five interconnected towers
still operate today, a rare example of Olympic-era hospitality
infrastructure that found a lasting second life rather than falling into
disuse after the event it was built for.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank7">
<div class="rank-number">7</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>Wynn Las Vegas &amp; Encore</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Las Vegas, Nevada, USA • <strong>4,748 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Wynn and its sister tower Encore represent the luxury end of the Las Vegas
mega-resort scale — proof that room count and five-star positioning
aren't mutually exclusive. Golf course, botanical gardens, high-end retail
and some of the Strip's top-rated restaurants all sit inside a property
still large enough to rank among the 10 biggest hotels on Earth.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank8">
<div class="rank-number">8</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>Barkhatnye Sezony</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Sochi, Russia • <strong>4,688 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Built along Sochi's Black Sea coastline, Barkhatnye Sezony ("Velvet
Seasons") is a resort-scale property developed to serve Russia's domestic
leisure and wellness tourism market, with a footprint that expanded
significantly around the 2014 Winter Olympics infrastructure buildout in
the region.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank9">
<div class="rank-number">9</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>Mandalay Bay Resort and Casino</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Las Vegas, Nevada, USA • <strong>4,426 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Anchoring the south end of the Las Vegas Strip, Mandalay Bay pairs its room
inventory with one of the largest convention centers in the city and a
signature beach-style pool complex complete with a wave pool —
positioning itself as a leisure resort and a MICE venue simultaneously,
inside the same property.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-entry" id="rank10">
<div class="rank-number">10</div>
<div class="rank-body">
<h3>Luxor Las Vegas</h3>
<p class="rank-meta">📍 Las Vegas, Nevada, USA • <strong>4,407 rooms</strong></p>
<p>
Instantly recognizable by its 30-story pyramid and the Sphinx replica at
its entrance, Luxor rounds out the list — and closes out a pattern
worth noticing: five of the ten biggest hotels in the world sit on a
single stretch of the Las Vegas Strip, a level of concentrated hospitality
infrastructure unmatched anywhere else on Earth.
</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
