</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section id="records">
<h2>Records Worth Knowing</h2>
<div class="card">
<h3>Largest Hotel Complex</h3>
<p>
The Venetian Resort (Las Vegas) — combining The Venetian, The
Palazzo and the Venetian North Tower — holds this title with 7,264
rooms spread across a connected complex, plus one of the largest
convention footprints in North America.
</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<h3>Largest Single Hotel Building</h3>
<p>
MGM Grand Las Vegas holds this distinction with 5,044 rooms under one
roof — the highest room count of any hotel built and operated as a
single, unified building rather than a connected complex of towers.
</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<h3>Guinness World Record Holder</h3>
<p>
First World Hotel is officially recognized by Guinness World Records as
the largest hotel in the world, with 7,351 rooms — the single
highest room count of any property on this list.
</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="operations">
<h2>Running a Hotel at This Scale</h2>
<p>
Numbers this large change the nature of the job. A 200-room hotel's
General Manager can walk the property and personally know most of the
long-tenured staff. At 5,000-plus rooms, that's structurally impossible
— the organization has to be built around systems, not individual
oversight.
</p>
<h3>What Changes at Mega-Resort Scale</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Housekeeping becomes an industrial operation.</strong> Turning over thousands of rooms in a single day requires shift-based room attendant fleets, dedicated linen logistics, and its own internal supply chain — not a single housekeeping department in the traditional sense.</li>
<li><strong>F&amp;B multiplies into dozens of outlets.</strong> A property this size doesn't run "the restaurant" — it runs a portfolio of independently managed outlets, each with its own P&amp;L, food cost targets and service standards, coordinated under one F&amp;B division.</li>
<li><strong>Staffing becomes a small city's workforce.</strong> Properties at this scale routinely employ 5,000 to 10,000+ staff across all departments — comparable to a mid-sized employer in an entire town, not a single business.</li>
<li><strong>Guest flow requires deliberate design.</strong> Casino resorts in particular design corridors, sightlines and amenity placement specifically to manage how tens of thousands of guests move through the property daily without bottlenecking.</li>
<li><strong>Technology stops being optional.</strong> Manual, paper-based processes that work fine at 150 rooms simply collapse at 5,000+ — PMS, revenue management systems and integrated POS become operational necessities, not upgrades.</li>
</ul>
<div class="tip">
<h3>Hospitality Insight</h3>
<p>
The leadership skill that scales best at this size isn't operational
knowledge — it's delegation architecture. A GM at a mega-resort
succeeds not by knowing every department's numbers personally, but by
building department heads capable of running their divisions like
independent businesses, all reporting into one coherent property strategy.
</p>
</div>
</section>
